 to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{8}{4 x - 1}.$ is true. Differentiating $f(x)=2 \\ln{\\left(4 x - 1 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(2 \\ln{\\left(4 x - 1 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=2\\cdot\\frac{4}{4 x - 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{8}{4 x - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=2 \\ln{\\left(4 x - 1 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{8}{4 x - 1}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}8 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{4 x - 1} &\\overset{?}{=}8e^{-(2 \\ln{\\left(4 x - 1 \\right)})/(2)} \\\\ \\frac{8}{4 x - 1} &\\overset{?}{=}8e^{-\\ln(4 x - 1)} \\\\ \\frac{8}{4 x - 1} &=\\frac{8}{4 x - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=2 \\ln{\\left(4 x - 1 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{8}{4 x - 1}.$; “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68427cdb-4aa1-5bed-8af1-4c431730eaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident