s defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}.$ is true. Differentiating $f(x)=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=2\\cdot\\frac{3}{3 x + 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}6 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{3 x + 1} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-(2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)})/(2)} \\\\ \\frac{6}{3 x + 1} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-\\ln(3 x + 1)} \\\\ \\frac{6}{3 x + 1} &=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}.$; “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41293d32-03d7-5bbb-9bcb-f371fea9293a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617136} {:answer/id #uuid "44849954-228a-571f-b0ed-a6efc2e38dcf" :answer